the death of another horse at California  Santa Anita racetrack is raising new  safety concerns about the sport in  Saturday's six million dollar Breeders  Cup Classic the horse Mongolian groom  broke his left hind leg and had to be  euthanized this is Santa Anita's 37th  horse death since December Don Dahler is  in Arcadia California Don this problem  just keeps getting bigger it really does  in a horse racing deaths though are a  national problem it's not just here in  consistent track surfaces the use of  performance-enhancing drugs as well as  over racing horses all those things  contribute and the fact that there is a  lack of a uniform regulatory body that  has the advocates for reform even more  concerned the exciting Breeders Cup  races Racing's unofficial world  championships had gone smoothly until  Saturday's final race nearly 68,000 fans  cheered a stirring win but soon realized  that four-year-old Mongolian groom had  suffered a serious injury near the end  of the race track officials and  veterinarians rushed to the injured  horse but after consultation with  several vets Mongolian groom was  euthanized protestors gathered outside  the track yesterday demanding reforms  for the sport that sees an estimated two  or three times as many deaths in the US  as other countries after the race PETA  responded forcefully calling for the  suspension of all trainers and  veterinarians of horses who died pending  full investigations among other reforms  we really have to transform the way  we've been racing last week we spoke  with veterinarian Deon Benson who was  hired to oversee protocols at Santa  Anita after it voluntarily closed last  spring her team now aggressively  monitors horses before they race  overseas new medication restrictions and  is hoping to bring new technology to  scan for hidden injuries  additionally the track has a new  drainage system it hasn't stopped deaths  entirely but they have slowed the  research says something along the lines  of ten horses a week died at racetracks  around this country is that just a part  of the industry that people maybe who  don't follow horse racing just don't  underst  dan I don't know that you they need to  understand it I think we need to get  better I think that if we can get the  rest of the country on the same page as  we are here at Santa Anita we can  improve that number advocacy groups are  asking Congress to establish a national  racing authority to regulate things such  as track conditions as well as the use  of medicines on these horses Gail Thank  You Don
 

